WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.262
Now we deliberately violate the scalar contract.

00:00:03.262 --> 00:00:07.267
The inner query returns many patient IDs from admissions.

00:00:07.267 --> 00:00:10.097
The outer expression expects one value.

00:00:10.097 --> 00:00:14.652
PostgreSQL cannot choose one row for you, so it raises an error.

00:00:14.652 --> 00:00:18.707
Do not automatically add LIMIT one just to remove the error.

00:00:18.707 --> 00:00:23.942
Only do that if choosing an arbitrary single row actually matches the business meaning.

00:00:23.942 --> 00:00:27.907
A subquery has a contract: it returns a shape.

00:00:27.907 --> 00:00:32.487
A scalar subquery must return at most one value where it is used.

00:00:32.487 --> 00:00:39.157
Before writing syntax, predict whether the inner query returns one value, one column with many rows,

00:00:39.157 --> 00:00:44.945
or a table. Shape mistakes are easier to prevent than to debug after the query becomes large.
